": [{"word": "\u0907\u0924\u093f", "end_index": 81, "start_index": 78}, {"word": "\u0930\u092e\u0924\u0947", "end_index": 169, "start_index": 165}, {"word": "\u0930\u093e\u092e\u0903", "end_index": 178, "start_index": 174}, {"word": "\u0930\u092e\u0903", "end_index": 182, "start_index": 179}, {"word": "\u0930\u0928\u094d\u0924\u093e", "end_index": 192, "start_index": 187}], "short_text": "Vyutpatti: \u092d\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0966-\u0906\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0966-\u0905\u0915\u0966-\u0905\u0928\u093f\u091f\u094d \u0964 \u0915\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0935\u0947\u091f\u094d \u0964\nMeaning: 1) \u0909 \u0919 \u091c \u0914 \u0915\u094d\u0930\u0940\u0921\u0947 \u0964 \u0907\u0924\u093f \u0915\u0935\u093f\u0915\u0932\u094d\u092a\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0941\u092e\u0903 {a...", "language": "Sanskrit", "source": "Sabdakalpadruma (Dhara Beta)", "src_short_title": "Sabdakalpadruma (Dhara Beta)", "source_url": "https://www.sanskrit-lexicon.uni-koeln.de/scans/SKDScan/2020/downloads/skdtxt.zip"}, {"text": "ram + a, I. adjective. 1. Pleasing, [Kir.] 5, 20 (at the end of a [comp.]). 2. Dear. II. masculine gender. 1. A husband, a lover. 2. The deity of love. III. feminine. m\u0101. 1. A wife or a mistress, [Pa\u00f1c.] i. [d.] 369 (?). 2. A name of Lak\u1e63m\u012b, [Pa\u00f1c.] 46, 8. -- [Comp.] Manas-, see s.v. manorama.", "dict_ref_id": 209600, "word_hyplinks": [{"word": "ram", "end_index": 3, "start_index": 0}, {"word": "ii", "end_index": 87, "start_index": 85}, {"word": "iii", "end_index": 155, "start_index": 152}, {"word": "m\u0101", "end_index": 169, "start_index": 167}, {"word": "lak\u1e63m\u012b", "end_index": 240, "start_index": 234}, {"word": "manorama", "end_index": 293, "start_index": 285}], "short_text": "ram + a, I. adjective. 1. Pleasing, [Kir.] 5, 20 (at the end of a [comp.]). 2. Dear. II. masculin...", "language": "English", "source": "A Sanskrit-English dictionary by BENFEY, T. (1866), with references to the best editions of Sanskrit authors and etymologies and comparisons of cognate words chiefly in Greek, Latin, Gothic, and Anglo-Saxon. London, Longmans, Green, and Co.", "src_short_title": "Skt-Eng Dict., BENFEY, T. (1866), London", "source_url": "https://www.sanskrit-lexicon.uni-koeln.de/scans/BENScan/2020/web/index.php"}, {"text": "ram-a, [adjective] ([\u2014\u00b0]) pleasing, delighting: \u0101, \ud83d\udf84[noun of the first declension, feminine] Epic, i. e. such\nworks as the Mah\u0101-bh\u0101-\nrata, R\u0101m\u0101ya\u1e47a, &c. of Lak\u1e63m\u012b; fortune, wealth; beauty; \ud83d\udf84-a\u1e47a, [adjective] (\u012b) pleasing, delighting; [noun of the first declension, masculine] lover, \ud83d\udf84husband; [noun of the first declension, neuter] pleasure; sexual enjoyment, \ud83d\udf84dalliance; gladdening; decoying (deer): \u012b, \ud83d\udf84[noun of the first declension, feminine] lovely young woman; mistress, wife.", "dict_ref_id": 313529, "word_hyplinks": [{"word": "rata", "end_index": 137, "start_index": 133}, {"word": "r\u0101m\u0101ya\u1e47a", "end_index": 147, "start_index": 139}, {"word": "lak\u1e63m\u012b", "end_index": 162, "start_index": 156}], "short_text": "ram-a, [adjective] ([\u2014\u00b0]) pleasing, delighting: \u0101, \ud83d\udf84[noun of the first declension, feminine] Epic...", "language": "English", "source": "A Sanskrit-English dictionary, MACDONELL, A. A. (1893), being a practical handbook with transliteration, accentuation, and etymological analysis throughout, London, Longmans, Green.", "src_short_title": "Skt-Eng Dict., MACDONELL, A. A. (1893), London", "source_url": "https://www.sanskrit-lexicon.uni-koeln.de/scans/MDScan/2020/web/index.php"}, {"text": "Rama is a village in Devgadh Baria Tehsil, Dohad District, Gujarat, India, with a pin code of 389130. It is located within the Gujarat Circle and Vadodara Region, part of the Panchmahals Division. Nearby railway stations include Bordi, and the nearest airport is Vadodara Airport. Another notable Rama is in Bhind Tehsil, Bhind District, Madhya Pradesh, India, with a pin code of 477555, situated within the Madhya Pradesh Circle, DivReportingCircle, and Morena Division, with Bhind Railway Station nearby and Gwalior Airport as the closest airport. Both locations feature temples and educational institutions, including schools and colleges, and are surrounded by various neighboring villages and cities.", "dict_ref_id": 10030558, "word_hyplinks": [], "short_text": "Rama is a village in Devgadh Baria Tehsil, Dohad District, Gujarat, India, with a pin code of 389...", "language": "English", "source": "Summary from the location Data", "src_short_title": "Location Summary", "source_url": null}]}, "similar_words": ["ram", "ram\u0101", "r\u0101ma", "r\u0101m\u0101", "rama\u1e25", "r\u0101ma\u1e25", "ramam", "r\u0101ma\u1e43", "Rima"]}}
{"type": "dhara_chunk", "item_id": 1, "data": [{"text": "Rama is a celebrated king and an avatar of Vishnu, known for his righteousness and valor [1], and is often depicted as a descendant of the sage Jamadagni [2]. He is recognized as a great warrior who, driven by anger, annihilated the entire tribe of Kshatriyas, forming five lakes of blood at Samanta-pancaka, which were later transformed into holy shrines [3]. As the son of Jamadagni, Rama is portrayed as a legendary hero skilled in arms and scripture [4], possessing exceptional martial abilities and mastering various weapons [5]. He is also described as a man of great patience, mental control, and multifarious vows [6], and is considered an illustrious scion of Bhrigu\u2019s race [7]. In some contexts, Rama is identified as Valadeva, appearing alongside Krishna and supporting the Pandavas [8], while in others, he is linked to the epic narrative involving the defeat of Ravana, crossing the ocean with the aid of monkeys, and ruling Ayodhya [9]. Additionally, there are accounts describing him as a historical figure whose story serves as inspiration for those in distress [10] and even as a metaphorical representation of divine intervention, such as when Vatapi assumes the form of a ram [11].\n\nReferences:  \n[1] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Adi Parva, Anukramanika Parva, Section I  \n[2] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Adi Parva, Sangraha Parva, Section II  \n[3] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Adi Parva, Sangraha Parva, Section II  \n[4] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Adi Parva, Astika Parva, Section LV  \n[5] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Adi Parva, Sambhava Parva, Section C  \n[6] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Adi Parva, Sambhava Parva, Section CXXXI  \n[7] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Adi Parva, Sambhava Parva, Section CIV  \n[8] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Adi Parva, Viduragamana Parva, Section CCVII  \n[9] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Vana Parva, Tirtha-yatra Parva, Section CXLVII  \n[10] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Adi Parva, Sambhava Parva, Section CXLII  \n[11] Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Vana Parva, Tirtha-yatra Parva, Section XCVI", "chunk_ref_id": 266318, "score": 0.7, "reference": "Dhara Summary for Rama", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Summary, Mah\u0101bh\u0101rata (English translation), Krishna-Dwaipayana Vy\u0101sa (tr. Kisari Mohan Ganguli), Bharata Press, Calcutta, 1883", "source_url": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/the-mahabharata-mohan/d/doc374926.html", "source_type": "merged augmentation", "chunk_misc": [{"title": "augmentations", "value": "{'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Anukramanika Parva->Section I, Rama is a celebrated king and an avatar of Vishnu, known for his righteousness and valor, mentioned among the great rulers of ancient times whose deeds and virtues are recorded in sacred texts.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Anukramanika Parva->Section I', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sangraha Parva->Section II, Rama is a warrior and a descendant of the sage Jamadagni. He is known for his valour and for annihilating the Kshatriyas in anger, which led to the formation of the five lakes of blood at Samanta-pancaka.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sangraha Parva->Section II', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sangraha Parva->Section II, Rama is the son of Jamadagni, a great warrior who, driven by anger, annihilated the entire tribe of Kshatriyas and formed five lakes of blood at Samanta-pancaka. He sought absolution for his sins and requested that the lakes become holy shrines, which they subsequently became.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sangraha Parva->Section II', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sangraha Parva->Section II, Rama is a legendary hero, the son of Jamadagni, known for his valor and righteousness. His story includes the annihilation of the Kshatriyas and the formation of lakes of blood, which later became holy shrines.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sangraha Parva->Section II', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Astika Parva->Section LV, Rama is compared to the son of Jamadagni, being conversant with the scriptures and arms.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Astika Parva->Section LV', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section LXVI, Rama is the youngest son of Jamadagni, superior to all his brothers in good qualities, skilful in all weapons, and the slayer of the Kshatriyas.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section LXVI', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section C, Rama is the son of Jamadagni and is renowned as a mighty bowman. His mastery of weapons is referenced in the context of Devavrata\u2019s extensive knowledge of all branches of warfare.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section C', \"According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section CIV, Rama is the son of Jamadagni who slew the king of the Haihayas in anger at his father's death. He cut off the thousand arms of Arjuna, the Haihaya king, and used his weapons to annihilate the Kshatriyas. He is described as an illustrious scion of Bhrigu\u2019s race.\": 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section CIV', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section CXXXI, Rama is the son of Jamadagnya and a scion of the Bhrigu race. He is described as a man of great patience, complete mental control, and mastery over weapons. He is also known as the exterminator of all foes and a man of multifarious vows.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section CXXXI', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section CXLII, Rama is referenced as a historical figure whose story is used to console those afflicted with sorrow. His tale, along with that of Nala, is suggested as a means to provide hope and solace to individuals in distress, emphasizing the value of recounting past examples of perseverance and triumph.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section CXLII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Caitraratha Parva->Section CLXVIII, Rama is a great Brahmana hero of Bhrigu\u2019s race who gave away all his wealth before retiring into the woods. He offered Drona either his body or his weapons, and Drona chose to receive all his weapons along with their use and withdrawal.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Caitraratha Parva->Section CLXVIII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Swayamvara Parva->Section CLXXXIX, Rama is Valadeva, who is present at the scene and exchanges a glance of satisfaction with Krishna.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Swayamvara Parva->Section CLXXXIX', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Swayamvara Parva->Section CLXL, Rama is the son of Jamadagni and is referenced as a legendary figure who defeated all Kshatriyas in battle. He is cited as an example of a Brahmana\u2019s capability to achieve great feats.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Swayamvara Parva->Section CLXL', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Swayamvara Parva->Section CLXLII, Rama is mentioned as a superhuman figure and a great warrior. Karna addresses Arjuna, questioning if he is Rama, suggesting that only such a powerful being could match Arjuna\u2019s strength in battle.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Swayamvara Parva->Section CLXLII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Viduragamana Parva->Section CCVII, Rama is a revered figure, referred to as Valadeva. He is mentioned as an ally of the Pandavas, highlighting their strength and righteousness.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Viduragamana Parva->Section CCVII', \"According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Viduragamana Parva->Section CCIX, Rama is a figure referred to as Valadeva and is mentioned alongside Krishna as a moral guide. He is part of the group that supports the Pandavas' return to their kingdom.\": 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Viduragamana Parva->Section CCIX', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Haranaharana Parva->Section CCXXIII, Rama is a companion of Kesava (Krishna) and one of the heroes of the Vrishni and Andhaka tribes. He is accompanied by Kesava to Indraprastha and is received by Yudhishthira with due ceremonies. Rama is also noted for his bravery and is part of the group of warriors who bring nuptial gifts to the Pandavas.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Haranaharana Parva->Section CCXXIII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Lokapala Sabhakhayana Parva->Section VIII, Rama is a king mentioned as present in the assembly house of Yama, along with other notable figures.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Lokapala Sabhakhayana Parva->Section VIII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Rajasuyarambha Parva->Section XIV, Rama is the son of Jamadagnya, known for exterminating a group of Kshatriyas and is referenced in the context of past heroic deeds.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Rajasuyarambha Parva->Section XIV', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Rajasuyarambha Parva->Section XV, Rama is a legendary warrior and a figure of great strength and virtue. He is mentioned alongside Krishna, Bhima, and Arjuna as a potential contender for defeating Jarasandha.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Rajasuyarambha Parva->Section XV', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Rajasuyika Parva->Section XXXIII, Rama is a mighty car-warrior who came to the Rajasuya sacrifice of Yudhishthira.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Rajasuyika Parva->Section XXXIII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Sisupala-badha Parva->Section LXXV, Rama is referenced as a figure who was tempted by a golden deer, used as an analogy to illustrate how men under calamity may lose their senses.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Sisupala-badha Parva->Section LXXV', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Sisupala-badha Parva->Section LXXVII, Rama is a sage who instructed Yudhishthira on the cliff of Bhrigu.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Sabha Parva->Sisupala-badha Parva->Section LXXVII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Arjunabhigamana Parva->Section XXV, Rama is the son of Dasaratha and is described as a sinless and mighty king, equal to Indra and Sakra in prowess. He is recalled by Markandeya as having lived in the forest at the command of his father, renouncing all pleasures for duty and righteousness.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Arjunabhigamana Parva->Section XXV', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Indralokagamana Parva->Section LI, Rama is a warrior who, along with Krishna, Bhima, Arjuna, and others, is promised to assist in slaying Duryodhana and his allies in battle.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Indralokagamana Parva->Section LI', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section LXXXIII, Rama is a heroic figure who exterminated the Kshatriyas, dug five lakes, and made them sacred tirthas, which are now revered for their spiritual merit.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section LXXXIII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section LXXXIV, Rama is a revered figure. With all his attendants and animals, he renounced his body and ascended to heaven in consequence of the efficacy of the tirtha alone.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section LXXXIV', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section LXXXVII, Rama is the son of Jamadagni, who recited the praises of his ancestry at the wood called Utpala, where Visvamitra had performed sacrifices.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section LXXXVII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section XCVI, Rama refers to a form assumed by Vatapi through illusion. Vatapi would transform into a ram, and after being cooked and offered to Brahmanas, he would re-embodied and emerge from their bodies, killing them.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section XCVI', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section XCIX, Rama is the son of Dasaratha, known for his spotless deeds and immense strength. He is also referred to as the manifestation of Vishnu\u2019s power and is described as stringing a celestial bow with ease.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section XCIX', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXV, Rama is a godlike warrior, son of Jamadagni, known for vanquishing the Haihaya king Arjuna and tracing his origin to Bhrigu\u2019s race.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXV', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXVI, Rama is the youngest and most virtuous son of Jamadagni and Renuka. He is commanded by his father to kill his mother after she is accused of losing her chastity. Rama obeys, beheads his mother, and later avenges his father\u2019s death by killing Kartavirya\u2019s son and defending his father\u2019s hermitage.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXVI', \"According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXVII, Rama is a warrior of immense strength and virtue who cremated his father, avenged his death by killing Kartavirya's sons, and subdued the entire military caste. He is described as a conqueror of hostile cities and a leader of great valor.\": 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXVII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXVIII, Rama is a leader of the Vrishni tribe. He, along with Krishna and others, came to visit Yudhishthira during his ascetic practices. He was honored by Yudhishthira and showed deep concern for the Pandavas\u2019 condition.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXVIII', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXX, Rama is a patron of the sons of Pritha, capable of protecting all three worlds. He is described as a powerful warrior who can surround the entire earth if roused. Rama is also referred to as a brother, friend, and preceptor to Arjuna, and is likened to the second self of Krishna.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXX', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXXX, Rama is a figure associated with creating a gap in a mountain, which is mentioned in the context of the sacred region of Vatikhanda.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXXX', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXLVI, Rama is the heroic son of Dasaratha, known as Vishnu\u2019s self in human form, who resides in the Dandaka forest with his queen and brother.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXLVI', 'According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXLVII, Rama is the son of Raghu who recovered his wife Sita and reigned in Ayodhya. He slew Ravana, the oppressor of the worlds, and installed Vibhishana as the ruler of Lanka. He crossed the ocean with the help of monkeys and is revered for his heroic deeds. Rama reigned for a thousand and ten hundred years before ascending to his own abode.': 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXLVII', \"According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXLIX, Rama is a heroic figure who encountered Ravana personally. His actions are praised by Hanuman, who explains that he refrained from killing Ravana to preserve Rama's glory and fame.\": 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Tirtha-yatra Parva->Section CXLIX', \"According to Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Markandeya-Samasya Parva->Section CCXIII, Rama is referenced as the son of Jamadagni, known for his devotion to his aged parents. He is used as a comparison to illustrate the fowler's similar level of piety and dedication towards his own parents.\": 'Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Vana Parva->Markandeya-Samasya Parva->Section CCXIII'}"}]}, {"text": "Rama is the son of King Dasaratha and Queen Kausalya, born in the family of Ikshvaku [1], and he is described as self-controlled, valorous, and illustrious, possessing heroic qualities and being equal to Brahma and Vishnu in his virtues [1]. As the central figure of the Ramayana, he is known for his righteousness and devotion [1], and is often regarded as truth incarnate, characterized by valour, benevolence, universal goodwill, clemency, pleasing looks, sweet disposition, love of truth, and humility [3]. He is also portrayed as a righteous king, a devoted son, and a hero who accomplishes great feats in the forest and elsewhere [3].\n\nRama is the King of Ayodhya and the father of Kusha and Lava, who listened to the Ramayana recited by his sons in his royal palace and praised their performance [4]. During various episodes, such as when he was asked to slay the yakshini Taraka, he demonstrated great valor and devotion by taking up his bow and killing her with arrows [6]. He received numerous divine weapons from Sage Vishvamitra, empowering him to conquer enemies and perform supernatural feats in battle [27], and showed respect and curiosity while learning how to use these weapons [28].\n\nAccompanied by his brother Lakshmana, Rama entered the hermitage of Kama upon performing morning devotions [23] and participated in several significant events under the guidance of Sage Vishvamitra, including protecting sacrifices and slaying demons [29\u201330]. His wisdom, curiosity, and reverence toward sages were evident throughout these journeys [45].\n\nIn addition to his martial exploits, Rama played a pivotal role in liberating Ahalya from the curse of Rishi Gautama [49] and was involved in the restoration of Shatananda\u2019s mother [51]. He was also present during key narratives concerning the descent of the Ganga [35\u201344], the conflicts surrounding King Trishanku [57\u201360], and the events related to King Ambarisha\u2019s sacrificial horse [61\u201362].\n\nHis most notable feat occurred when he broke the divine bow of King Janaka, showcasing unparalleled strength and earning the hand of Princess Sita in marriage [67]. This event marked him as a central figure in the wedding ceremony, where he was adorned with ornaments and celebrated as a divine figure [73]. Additionally, he faced challenges from figures like Parasurama, further establishing his reputation as a valiant prince and a formidable warrior [75].\n\nReferences:  \n[1] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 1 - Shri Narada relates to Valmiki the story of Rama  \n[2] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 2 - Sage Valmiki creates the metrical form for the story  \n[3] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 3 - The deeds of Rama that will be described in the sacred poem  \n[4] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 4 - Shri Rama\u2019s sons chant the poem  \n[6] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 26 - How the Yakshini Taraka was slain  \n[27] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 27 - Shri Rama is given the celestial weapons  \n[28] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 28 - Shri Rama is instructed in their use  \n[23] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 23 - Ramachandra and Lakshmana reach the hermitage of Kama  \n[29] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 29 - Vishvamitra relates the story of his hermitage  \n[30] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 30 - Maricha and Suvahu obstruct the sacrifice and are slain by Rama  \n[45] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 45 - The city of Vishala and the churning of the ocean  \n[49] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 49 - Shri Rama liberates Ahalya from Gautama\u2019s curse  \n[51] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 51 - Gautama\u2019s son Shatananda  \n[35] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 35 - The origin of the holy river Ganga  \n[44] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 44 - King Bhagiratha completes the funeral rites for his ancestors  \n[57] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 57 - Shri Vasishtha refuses to help King Trishanku  \n[60] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 60 - King Trishanku ascends to a specially created heaven  \n[61] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 61 - King Ambarisha\u2019s sacrificial horse is lost  \n[67] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 67 - Rama breaks the bow and is given the Princess Sita in marriage  \n[73] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 73 - The marriage ceremonies are completed  \n[75] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 75 - Parasurama challenges Rama to combat", "chunk_ref_id": 4, "score": 0.57, "reference": "Dhara Summary for Rama", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Summary, The Ramayana of Valmiki (Shastri): An English Translation, Hari Prasad Shastri, Shanti Sadan, London, 1952", "source_url": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/the-ramayana-of-valmiki", "source_type": "merged augmentation", "chunk_misc": [{"title": "augmentations", "value": "{'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 1 - Shri Narada relates to Valmiki the story of Rama, Rama is the son of King Dasaratha and Queen Kausalya, born in the family of Ikshvaku. He is described as self-controlled, valorous, and illustrious, possessing heroic qualities and being equal to Brahma and Vishnu in his virtues. He is the central figure of the Ramayana, known for his righteousness and devotion.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 1 - Shri Narada relates to Valmiki the story of Rama', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 2 - Sage Valmiki creates the metrical form for the story, Rama is the central figure of the epic, described as the essence of virtue and full of the highest attributes. Valmiki is inspired to compose his story in verse, which becomes a work of infinite merit.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 2 - Sage Valmiki creates the metrical form for the story', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 3 - The deeds of Rama that will be described in the sacred poem, Rama is truth incarnate, known for his valour, benevolence, universal goodwill, clemency, pleasing looks, sweet disposition, love of truth, and humility. He is the central figure of the sacred poem, whose life and deeds are described in detail by Sage Valmiki. He is depicted as a righteous king, a devoted son, and a hero who accomplishes great feats in the forest and other places.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 3 - The deeds of Rama that will be described in the sacred poem', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 4 - Shri Rama\u2019s sons chant the poem, Rama is the King of Ayodhya and the father of Kusha and Lava, who listened to the Ramayana recited by his sons in his royal palace and praised their performance.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 4 - Shri Rama\u2019s sons chant the poem', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 20 - Dasaratha\u2019s reluctance, Rama is another name for Shri Rama, the virtuous eldest son of Dasaratha. Dasaratha refuses to let him fight the rakshasas due to his youth and inexperience.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 20 - Dasaratha\u2019s reluctance', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 23 - Ramachandra and Lakshmana reach the hermitage of Kama, Rama is the son of Queen Kaushalya, addressed by Muni Vishvamitra, and performs morning devotions with Lakshmana before reaching the hermitage of Kama.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 23 - Ramachandra and Lakshmana reach the hermitage of Kama', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 24 - The dark forest of Taraka, Rama is a royal prince who, along with his brother, is accompanied by the sage Vishvamitra. He is tasked with slaying the wicked yakshini Taraka to free the land from her evil designs and restore peace to the uninhabited forest.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 24 - The dark forest of Taraka', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 25 - Vishvamitra seeks to convince Rama, Rama is a prince of immense power and influence who is urged by Vishvamitra to slay Taraka. He questions the strength of Taraka and listens to the sage's explanation of her origin and curse. Rama is portrayed as a righteous warrior bound by duty.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 25 - Vishvamitra seeks to convince Rama', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 26 - How the Yakshini Taraka was slain, Rama is the son of Dasaratha, who listens to the sage Vishvamitra, takes up his bow, and slays the yakshini Taraka with arrows, showing great valor and devotion.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 26 - How the Yakshini Taraka was slain', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 27 - Shri Rama is given the celestial weapons, Rama is a mighty prince and the recipient of numerous divine weapons from Sage Vishvamitra, which empower him to conquer enemies and perform supernatural feats in battle.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 27 - Shri Rama is given the celestial weapons', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 28 - Shri Rama is instructed in their use, Rama is another name for Shri Rama, referring to the same revered prince who receives divine weapons and instructions from a holy sage. He is shown to be respectful and inquisitive, seeking guidance on how to use and withdraw the weapons.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 28 - Shri Rama is instructed in their use', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 29 - Vishvamitra relates the story of his hermitage, Rama is the illustrious prince, accompanied by Lakshmana, who enters the Siddha-ashrama with Vishvamitra and is requested to slay the rakshasas.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 29 - Vishvamitra relates the story of his hermitage', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 30 - Maricha and Suvahu obstruct the sacrifice and are slain by Rama, Rama is referred to as the Mighty-armed Prince who obeys his Guru's commands and successfully protects the sacrifice. He is worshipped by the sages as a victorious Indra.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 30 - Maricha and Suvahu obstruct the sacrifice and are slain by Rama', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 31 - Vishvamitra starts out, Rama is the great hero and ever-cheerful prince who accompanies Lakshmana on a journey with Vishvamitra. He shows humility and obedience to the sage and later inquires about the country they are in, covered with verdant groves.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 31 - Vishvamitra starts out', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 32 - Vishvamitra tells of his ancestors and the dynasty of King Kusha, Rama is a revered figure addressed by King Kusha and others. He is referred to as the Prince of Raghu and is a central figure in the narrative, though his direct role in the story is not elaborated.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 32 - Vishvamitra tells of his ancestors and the dynasty of King Kusha', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 34, Rama is addressed by Vishvamitra during the narration of his lineage and the story of his family, including his father Gadhi.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 34 - Gadhi is the father of Vishvamitra', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 35 - The origin of the holy river Ganga, Rama is Prince Rama, who listens to Vishvamitra's instructions and seeks knowledge about the origin of the holy river Ganga.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 35 - The origin of the holy river Ganga', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 37 - The king\u2019s elder daughter, Ganga, Rama is a revered figure to whom the narrative is addressed. He is informed of the story of Ganga and Karttikeya, and is told that those who read it with faith will gain blessings.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 37 - The king\u2019s elder daughter, Ganga', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 40 - Sagara\u2019s sons search for the horse, Rama is a title or address used by the narrators to refer to the listener, likely a descendant of King Raghu, during the recounting of the story of Sagara\u2019s sons.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 40 - Sagara\u2019s sons search for the horse', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 42 - Bhagiratha performs austerities, Rama is a revered figure addressed in the narrative, used to denote the glory of kings like Amshuman and the significance of the Ganga's descent.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 42 - Bhagiratha performs austerities', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 43, Rama is the listener or addressee of the narrative, to whom the story of the descent of the Ganga is recounted.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 43 - Lord Shiva lets loose the sacred river', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 44 - King Bhagiratha completes the funeral rites for his ancestors, Rama is the listener to the story of the descent of the Ganga, to whom the narrative is recounted by Shri Brahma, emphasizing the spiritual and moral benefits of hearing the tale.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 44 - King Bhagiratha completes the funeral rites for his ancestors', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 45 - The city of Vishala and the churning of the ocean, Rama is the prince who listens to the story of Vishala and the churning of the ocean with Lakshmana. He is portrayed as wise, curious, and respectful of the sage Vishvamitra.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 45 - The city of Vishala and the churning of the ocean', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 46 - Diti undergoes severe austerities for the birth of a son, Rama is a revered figure addressed by Diti in her plea to Kasyapa. He is invoked as a witness to her anguish and her request for a son capable of destroying Indra.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 46 - Diti undergoes severe austerities for the birth of a son', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 47 - The holy sage and the princes arrive at Vishala, Rama is addressed by the narrator as 'O Rama!' and is referred to as the 'Lion among Men.' He is the listener to the story of the holy sage and the princes arriving at Vishala.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 47 - The holy sage and the princes arrive at Vishala', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 49 - Shri Rama liberates Ahalya from Gautama\u2019s curse, Rama is the divine hero who liberates Ahalya from the curse of Rishi Gautama and departs for Mithila after restoring her to her former state.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 49 - Shri Rama liberates Ahalya from Gautama\u2019s curse', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 51 - Gautama\u2019s son Shatananda, Rama is a great and enlightened prince, protected by Sage Vishvamitra, who restores the former state of Shatananda\u2019s mother.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 51 - Gautama\u2019s son Shatananda', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 54, Rama is addressed as O Rama, indicating his role as a central figure in the narrative, likely a prince or hero observing the conflict between Shabala and Vishvamitra.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 54 - King Vishvamitra attempts to carry Shabala away by force', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 57 - Shri Vasishtha refuses to help King Trishanku, Rama is mentioned in the context of accompanying Vishvamitra on his ascetic penance. He is referred to as the husband of Sita and is associated with the spiritual and moral endeavors of the sages.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 57 - Shri Vasishtha refuses to help King Trishanku', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 58 - Vasishtha\u2019s sons curse Trishanku, Rama is a figure whose words provoke the sons of Shri Vasishtha, leading them to curse King Trishanku for his perceived ignorance and defiance.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 58 - Vasishtha\u2019s sons curse Trishanku', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 60 - King Trishanku ascends to a specially created heaven, Rama is the listener or addressee of the narrative, to whom the events of the sacrifice and creation of a new heaven for King Trishanku are recounted.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 60 - King Trishanku ascends to a specially created heaven', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 61 - King Ambarisha\u2019s sacrificial horse is lost, Rama is invoked by Vishvamitra as a witness to the events involving King Ambarisha\u2019s sacrificial horse and the subsequent search for a human victim.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 61 - King Ambarisha\u2019s sacrificial horse is lost', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 62 - Shunashepha obtains help from Vishvamitra, Rama is a revered figure to whom the narrative is addressed. The story of Shunashepha and Vishvamitra is recounted in his honor, highlighting the events involving King Ambarisha and the completion of the sacrifice.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 62 - Shunashepha obtains help from Vishvamitra', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 63 - Vishvamitra is proclaimed a Maharishi, Rama is addressed by Vishvamitra during the narrative, indicating his presence as a listener or participant in the events described.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 63 - Vishvamitra is proclaimed a Maharishi', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 65 - Vishvamitra performs another thousand years\u2019 austerities, Rama is a central figure present during Vishvamitra\u2019s narrative. He is accompanied by Lakshmana and is honored by King Janaka, who requests Vishvamitra\u2019s presence at his sacrifice. Rama is referred to as Raghava and is respected by the sages.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 65 - Vishvamitra performs another thousand years\u2019 austerities', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 67 - Rama breaks the bow and is given the Princess Sita in marriage, Rama is the illustrious prince who breaks the divine bow, demonstrating unparalleled strength. He is addressed by King Janaka and Sage Vishvamitra, and his action leads to his marriage with Princess Sita. Rama is described as the son of King Dasaratha and is revered as a divine figure.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 67 - Rama breaks the bow and is given the Princess Sita in marriage', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 70 - Vishvamitra relates the descent of the dynasty, Rama is one of the two sons of King Dasaratha and is requested by Dasaratha to be given the hand of King Janaka\u2019s daughter in marriage. He is part of the lineage of King Ikshvaku, tracing back to the Unmanifest Brahma.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 70 - Vishvamitra relates the descent of the dynasty', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 73, Rama is the eldest prince of King Dasaratha and is married to Sita during the nuptial ceremony. He is the central figure of the ceremony and is adorned with ornaments during the sacred rites.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 73 - The marriage ceremonies are completed', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 74 - Parasurama appears amidst inauspicious signs, Rama is a revered figure addressed by Parasurama after accepting traditional offerings, and is also referred to as Shri Rama.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 74 - Parasurama appears amidst inauspicious signs', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 75 - Parasurama challenges Rama to combat, Rama is an illustrious hero known for his great prowess and the heroic deed of breaking the bow at Janakapura. He is challenged by Parasurama to demonstrate his strength by drawing the terrible bow Yamadagni. Rama is referred to as a valiant prince and is the son of King Dasaratha.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 75 - Parasurama challenges Rama to combat'}"}]}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 6.28, Rama is the king of Ayodhya, a dark-complexioned figure with lotus eyes, known for his righteousness, mastery of weapons, and devotion to dharma. He is advancing to wage war against Ravana.", "chunk_ref_id": 45047, "score": 0.5, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->6.28", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 3.69, Rama is a prince of the Ikshvaku race and the son of Dasaratha. He is a mighty hero who, along with his brother Lakshmana, searches for Sita in the dense forest. He is described as courageous and of fiery splendour, and he remains composed even in the face of danger.", "chunk_ref_id": 41353, "score": 0.48, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->3.69", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 3.47, Rama is the husband of Sita, a virtuous, truthful, and self-controlled king of the Kakutstha dynasty. He is described as large-eyed, long-armed, and a refuge to all beings. He is banished to the forest for fourteen years due to Kaikeyi's boon.", "chunk_ref_id": 41020, "score": 0.45, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->3.47", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Ithihasa, Mahabharata, Adi Parva, Sambhava Parva, Section CXXXI, Rama is the son of Jamadagnya and a scion of the Bhrigu race. He is described as a man of great patience, complete mental control, and mastery over weapons. He is also known as the exterminator of all foes and a man of multifarious vows.", "chunk_ref_id": 283150, "score": 0.45, "reference": "Ithihasa->Mahabharata->Adi Parva->Sambhava Parva->Section CXXXI", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Mah\u0101bh\u0101rata (English translation), Krishna-Dwaipayana Vy\u0101sa (tr. Kisari Mohan Ganguli), Bharata Press, Calcutta, 1883", "source_url": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/the-mahabharata-mohan/d/doc374926.html", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 2.48, Rama is the scion of the Kakutstha race and the son of Dasaratha. He is described as a tiger among men, a subduer of enemies, and a great charioteer. His presence brings grace to the forest, and he is revered as the supreme refuge and protector of people. He was deceitfully exiled along with Sita and Lakshmana.", "chunk_ref_id": 39674, "score": 0.44, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->2.48", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 6.95, Rama is a divine hero and the protagonist of the Ramayana. He is portrayed as an invincible warrior who destroys thousands of Rakshasas in battle with his sharp, glowing arrows. Rama is described as tireless, virtuous, and capable of immense strength, often referred to as Sri Rama or Raghava.", "chunk_ref_id": 46805, "score": 0.39, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->6.95", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 5.34, Rama is the son of Dasaratha, master of the Vedas, wielder of Brahmastra, and the heroic king who is separated from Sita. He is described as truthful, prosperous, and the king of kings, destined to defeat Ravana.", "chunk_ref_id": 43148, "score": 0.39, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->5.34", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 5.35, Rama is the husband of Sita and the son of Dasaratha. He is described as a protector of the world, a paragon of virtue, and a warrior of great strength and intelligence. He is known for his righteousness, self-control, and devotion to dharma.", "chunk_ref_id": 43168, "score": 0.38, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->5.35", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 3.37, Rama is the son of Kausalya, a virtuous and brave king comparable to Indra and Varuna. He is dharma incarnate, pious, and truthful, and is described as the king of all worlds. He is not harsh, ignorant, or cruel, and is protected by the strength of his character. Rama is the husband of Sita and is likened to a lion in strength and courage.", "chunk_ref_id": 40889, "score": 0.36, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->3.37", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 2.118, Rama is the heroic and righteous prince of Ayodhya, son of King Dasaratha. He is known for his compassion, adherence to dharma, and valour. He wins Sita's hand in a swayamvara by bending a divine bow. He treats all women with respect and affection.", "chunk_ref_id": 39126, "score": 0.35, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->2.118", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 1.27, Rama is a prince of great renown, descendant of Raghu, and a tiger among men. He is the recipient of celestial weapons from Viswamitra. He is described as mighty-armed, heroic, and blemishless, and he commands the weapons to live in his mind.", "chunk_ref_id": 37723, "score": 0.34, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->1.27", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 6.28, Rama is a king of the Ikshvaku race, known for his righteousness, skill in warfare, and use of Vedic knowledge. He is advancing to wage war with Ravana and is described as having a golden-radiating body and red eyes.", "chunk_ref_id": 45027, "score": 0.32, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->6.28", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 2.86, Rama is the exiled prince, son of Dasaratha, who is described as virtuous, resembling his father in many ways, and destined to bring righteousness to the world. He is protected by Lakshmana and is the central figure of the narrative.", "chunk_ref_id": 40254, "score": 0.31, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->2.86", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 2.83, Rama is the mighty-armed hero of dark-blue complexion, firm in strength and steadfast in vows, who is the destroyer of the sorrows of the world. He is the object of longing for Bharata and the subject of admiration and joy for the people. His achievements are narrated by those who follow Bharata on their journey.", "chunk_ref_id": 40214, "score": 0.31, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->2.83", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 5.21, Rama is the righteous and powerful prince, husband of Sita, and the son of King Dasharatha. He is portrayed as a dharma-bearer and a compassionate ruler. Sita urges Ravana to seek friendship with Rama to avoid death, as Rama's wrath is unstoppable.", "chunk_ref_id": 42926, "score": 0.31, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->5.21", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "Shri Rama is the central figure of the sacred poem [1], known for his righteousness, valour, and devotion, and is described as the divine being for whom celestial beings incarnate as warriors of the monkey tribe are created [3]. He is the King of Ayodhya who listens to the *Ramayana* recited by his sons, Kusha and Lava, and praises their performance [2]; he is also the lotus-eyed, virtuous eldest son of Dasaratha [5] and one of the four sons of King Dasaratha, in perfect accord with Sita [12]. As a revered prince, he receives divine weapons and instructions from a holy sage [6], demonstrates respect and inquisitiveness, and is honored by sages such as Vishvamitra [11]. He is also the eternal Purusha and Lord Vishnu himself, descending to Earth to slay Ravana [17], and is regarded as the Protectors of the World, embodying unparalleled virtues, valour, and integrity [17].\n\nRama is the son of King Dasaratha and Queen Kaushalya [18], destined for kingship but forced into exile due to Queen Kaikeyi\u2019s demands [19]. During this time, he fulfills his father\u2019s decree, showing unwavering obedience to dharma and filial duty [21], and is described as virtuous, compassionate, and resolute [24]. He is accompanied by Sita and Lakshmana during his exile [30], and prior to leaving, he distributes his wealth generously to brahmins, friends, and servants [32]. Upon departing Ayodhya, he is mourned by the entire city, seen as a godlike figure bound by duty [40], and is remembered as the Upholder of Truth, bringing peace and prosperity wherever he resides [44].\n\nDuring his journey into the forest, he is guided by Sumantra and others, reflecting on his family\u2019s suffering while maintaining composure and determination [46]. He is also celebrated as the one who liberated Ahalya from Gautama\u2019s curse [9], and is recognized as a fearsome yet restrained warrior capable of confronting figures like Parasurama [16]. Ultimately, he is destined to reclaim the throne after completing his trials and restoring justice [44].\n\nReferences:  \n[1] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 3 - The deeds of Rama that will be described in the sacred poem  \n[2] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 4 - Shri Rama\u2019s sons chant the poem  \n[3] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 17 - Celestial beings incarnate as warriors of the monkey tribe  \n[5] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 20 - Dasaratha\u2019s reluctance  \n[6] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 28 - Shri Rama is instructed in their use  \n[9] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 49 - Shri Rama liberates Ahalya from Gautama\u2019s curse  \n[11] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 51 - Gautama\u2019s son Shatananda  \n[12] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 72 - The marriage of the four sons of King Dasaratha  \n[16] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Bala Kanda, Chapter 76 - Parasurama is vanquished and deprived of his glory and power  \n[17] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Ayodhya Kanda, Chapter 1 - King Dasaratha desires to see Prince Rama made regent  \n[18] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Ayodhya Kanda, Chapter 7 - Shri Rama\u2019s coming installation  \n[19] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Ayodhya Kanda, Chapter 9 - Queen Kaikeyi is resolved upon her evil design  \n[21] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Ayodhya Kanda, Chapter 21 - Shri Rama prepares for departure  \n[24] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Ayodhya Kanda, Chapter 24 - Shri Rama\u2019s resolution  \n[30] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Ayodhya Kanda, Chapter 30 - Seeing Sita\u2019s fixed resolve Shri Rama grants her request  \n[32] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Ayodhya Kanda, Chapter 32 - Shri Rama bestows his wealth  \n[40] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Ayodhya Kanda, Chapter 40 - All Ayodhya is distressed to see Shri Rama\u2019s chariot depart  \n[44] Ithihasa, Ramayana, Ayodhya Kanda, Chapter 44 - Queen Kaushalya finds peace in the consolation of Queen Sumitra", "chunk_ref_id": 49, "score": 0.3, "reference": "Dhara Summary for Shri Rama", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Summary, The Ramayana of Valmiki (Shastri): An English Translation, Hari Prasad Shastri, Shanti Sadan, London, 1952", "source_url": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/the-ramayana-of-valmiki", "source_type": "merged augmentation", "chunk_misc": [{"title": "augmentations", "value": "{'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 3 - The deeds of Rama that will be described in the sacred poem, Shri Rama is the central figure of the sacred poem, known for his righteousness, valour, and devotion. His life, including his exile, the abduction of Sita, and the eventual defeat of Ravana, is described in detail by Sage Valmiki.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 3 - The deeds of Rama that will be described in the sacred poem', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 4 - Shri Rama\u2019s sons chant the poem, Shri Rama is the King of Ayodhya who listened to the Ramayana recited by his sons Kusha and Lava. He was pleased with their performance and praised their musical and poetic skill.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 4 - Shri Rama\u2019s sons chant the poem', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 4 - Shri Rama\u2019s sons chant the poem, Shri Rama is the King of Ayodhya who listens to the Ramayana recited by his sons, Kusha and Lava, and praises their performance in his royal assembly.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 4 - Shri Rama\u2019s sons chant the poem', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 17 - Celestial beings incarnate as warriors of the monkey tribe, Shri Rama is the divine being for whom the celestial beings incarnate as warriors of the monkey tribe are created. He is assisted by these warriors in his just undertaking for the good of all.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 17 - Celestial beings incarnate as warriors of the monkey tribe', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 20 - Dasaratha\u2019s reluctance, Shri Rama is the lotus-eyed, virtuous eldest son of Dasaratha. He is described as a young child without military experience, and Dasaratha refuses to let him fight the rakshasas due to his inexperience.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 20 - Dasaratha\u2019s reluctance', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 28 - Shri Rama is instructed in their use, Shri Rama is a revered prince who receives divine weapons and instructions from a holy sage. He is shown to be respectful and inquisitive, seeking guidance on how to use and withdraw the weapons. He is addressed by deities and is considered worthy of possessing powerful divine arms.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 28 - Shri Rama is instructed in their use', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 36, Shri Rama is one of the princes who questions Shri Vishvamitra about the elder daughter of the King of Himalaya and the origin of the Gunga river.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 36 - The story of the king of Himalayas\u2019 younger daughter Uma', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 39, Shri Rama is a prince who listens to the tale of King Sagara's sacrifice and asks Muni Vishvamitra about it.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 39 - The horse with which Sagara performs a sacrifice is stolen', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 49 - Shri Rama liberates Ahalya from Gautama\u2019s curse, Shri Rama is the protagonist who liberates Ahalya from Gautama\u2019s curse and departs for Mithila. He is revered as the glorious Rama, accepted homage from Ahalya, and is described as the one who restores her to her former state.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 49 - Shri Rama liberates Ahalya from Gautama\u2019s curse', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 50 - The place of sacrifice by King Janaka, Shri Rama is one of the two illustrious princes accompanied by Lakshmana. He is described as valiant, youthful, and resembling the gods in bearing. He is identified by Shri Vishvamitra as the son of King Dasaratha.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 50 - The place of sacrifice by King Janaka', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 51 - Gautama\u2019s son Shatananda, Shri Rama is a revered and mighty prince, honored by Sage Vishvamitra and the subject of inquiry by Shatananda.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 51 - Gautama\u2019s son Shatananda', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 72 - The marriage of the four sons of King Dasaratha, Shri Rama is one of the four sons of King Dasaratha. He is in perfect accord with Sita, and both are described as equals in grace and heritage.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 72 - The marriage of the four sons of King Dasaratha', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 76 - Parasurama is vanquished and deprived of his glory and power, Shri Rama is a kshatriya and a descendant of the Solar race. He is portrayed as a valiant warrior who confronts Parasurama with restraint and defiance. He wields a divine arrow from Vishnu, demonstrating his prowess and divine connection. He is also referred to as the Imperishable Vishnu and the Son of Raghu.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Bala Kanda->Chapter 76 - Parasurama is vanquished and deprived of his glory and power', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 1 - King Dasaratha desires to see Prince Rama made regent, Shri Rama is the eternal Purusha and Lord Vishnu himself, who descended on earth to slay Ravana and is described as possessing unparalleled virtues, valour, and integrity.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 1 - King Dasaratha desires to see Prince Rama made regent', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 7 - Shri Rama\u2019s coming installation, Shri Rama is the son of King Dasaratha and Queen Kaushalya. He is about to be installed as Yuvaraja, the crown prince, under the Pushya star. His installation is a major event in Ayodhya, marked by widespread celebrations and auspicious activities.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 7 - Shri Rama\u2019s coming installation', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 9 - Queen Kaikeyi is resolved upon her evil design, Shri Rama is the revered prince destined for kingship. His enthronement is to be halted by Queen Kaikeyi\u2019s demands, and his exile is planned to secure Bharata\u2019s rule. He is described as a figure of great importance and affection.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 9 - Queen Kaikeyi is resolved upon her evil design', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 10 - King Dasaratha is deeply afflicted, Shri Rama is the subject of the king's instructions for installation. The king has given orders for the installation of Shri Rama before entering the inner apartments of the queens.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 10 - King Dasaratha is deeply afflicted', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 16 - Shri Rama in his chariot drives swiftly to the king, Shri Rama is the prince and future king of Ayodhya, depicted as virtuous, resplendent, and destined for coronation, accompanied by Sumantra and Lakshmana.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 16 - Shri Rama in his chariot drives swiftly to the king', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 18 - Shri Rama sees the king full of anguish and speechless, Shri Rama is the son of King Dasaratha and is deeply distressed upon seeing his father's anguish. He is respectful, devoted, and seeks to understand the cause of his father's sorrow. He is willing to obey any command from his father, even at great personal cost.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 18 - Shri Rama sees the king full of anguish and speechless', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 20 - Queen Kaushalya is afflicted and helpless with sorrow, Shri Rama is the prince who is about to leave for exile, revered for his righteousness, compassion, and his role as the sole support of all beings.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 20 - Queen Kaushalya is afflicted and helpless with sorrow', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 21 - Shri Rama prepares for departure, Shri Rama is the righteous and virtuous prince who is commanded by his father to go into exile. He is described as guileless, self-controlled, and forbearing, and he is determined to obey his father\u2019s commands despite the grief of his mother and brother. He prepares for departure, emphasizing his commitment to dharma and the traditions of his royal lineage.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 21 - Shri Rama prepares for departure', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 23 - Shri Lakshmana and Shri Rama\u2019s installation, Shri Rama is the elder brother of Lakshmana and the rightful heir to the throne. He is about to be exiled due to a royal decree, and he is urged by Lakshmana to resist it. Rama ultimately chooses to obey his father\u2019s will, prioritizing dharma and filial duty over his own desires.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 23 - Shri Lakshmana and Shri Rama\u2019s installation', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 24 - Shri Rama\u2019s resolution, Shri Rama is the righteous and virtuous son of King Dasaratha, who is determined to obey his father\u2019s command to live in the forest. He reassures his mother, Queen Kaushalya, of his commitment to dharma and advises her to remain with the king. He emphasizes the importance of serving one\u2019s husband and upholding eternal dharma. Shri Rama is portrayed as a figure of devotion, duty, and emotional restraint.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 24 - Shri Rama\u2019s resolution', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 30 - Seeing Sita\u2019s fixed resolve Shri Rama grants her request, Shri Rama is a prince who warns Sita of the dangers of the forest and initially refuses to let her accompany him. He is described as a sinless prince and is deeply moved by Sita\u2019s devotion. He ultimately agrees to take her with him after being convinced by her resolve. He is also referred to as a fearless and virtuous figure who obeys his father\u2019s command.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 30 - Seeing Sita\u2019s fixed resolve Shri Rama grants her request', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 32 - Shri Rama bestows his wealth, Shri Rama is a prince who is about to enter the forest and bestows his wealth upon brahmins, friends, and servants. He is described as having a sweet speech and is shown bestowing gifts such as ornaments, jewels, and animals. He also gives alms to the poor and honors brahmins with generous gifts.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 32 - Shri Rama bestows his wealth', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 33 - Shri Rama goes with Sita and Lakshmana to King Dasaratha\u2019s palace, Shri Rama is a virtuous and compassionate king who, after distributing riches to brahmins, goes with Sita and Lakshmana to visit his father, King Dasaratha. He is described as the Lord of the world, the knower of dharma, and the root of a tree whose people are his flowers, fruit, leaves, and branches. He is portrayed as innocent, truthful, and self-controlled, and his absence causes great distress among his subjects.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 33 - Shri Rama goes with Sita and Lakshmana to King Dasaratha\u2019s palace', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 34 - King Dasaratha gives his blessing, Shri Rama is the respectful title used for Rama, the prince who is about to leave for the forest. He is brought before the king by Sumantra and is described as clothed with every excellent quality.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 34 - King Dasaratha gives his blessing', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 36, Shri Rama is the prince who is ordered into exile by Kaikeyi. He is to be accompanied by an army, attendants, and supplies, and is expected to live in the forest while observing sacrifices and bestowing alms.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 36 - Queen Kaikeyi disregards the words of the chief minister and the king', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 40 - All Ayodhya is distressed to see Shri Rama\u2019s chariot depart, Shri Rama is the exiled prince who departs Ayodhya with Sita and Lakshmana, fulfilling his father\u2019s decree. He is described as godlike, compassionate, and bound by duty. He is accompanied by Sumantra on a chariot adorned with gold and weapons. His departure causes great distress among the people of Ayodhya.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 40 - All Ayodhya is distressed to see Shri Rama\u2019s chariot depart', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 41 - The whole world grieves for Prince Rama, Shri Rama is the virtuous and truthful prince, known as the Protector of the World, who was exiled by his father due to Kaikeyi's influence, causing great distress among the people of Ayodhya.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 41 - The whole world grieves for Prince Rama', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 43 - The lament of Queen Kaushalya, Shri Rama is the exiled son of King Dasharatha, known as the subduer of foes. He is described as the long-armed wielder of the great bow, who has been sent to the forest with Sita and Lakshmana. Queen Kaushalya longs for his return to Ayodhya.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 43 - The lament of Queen Kaushalya', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 44, Shri Rama is the virtuous and righteous son of Queen Kaushalya, who renounces the throne to honor his father and follow the path of dharma. He is described as the protector of all beings, endowed with great prowess, and destined to reclaim the kingdom after his exile. His presence is said to bring peace and prosperity.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 44 - Queen Kaushalya finds peace in the consolation of Queen Sumitra', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 45 - The lament of the brahmins who follow Shri Rama, Shri Rama is the Upholder of Truth, revered by the people of Ayodhya for his virtues, and is described as resplendent like the full moon. He is compassionate, determined, and devoted to dharma, and he instructs the people to honor Bharata in his absence. He is followed by the citizens of Ayodhya as he journeys to the forest.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 45 - The lament of the brahmins who follow Shri Rama', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 46 - Shri Rama drives on the forest, Shri Rama is the exiled prince who, with Sita and Lakshmana, travels to the forest. He reassures his brother Lakshmana about their exile and reflects on his parents' distress. He also instructs the charioteer Sumantra to prepare the chariot and later directs him on the route to take. He is described as a virtuous and dutiful prince.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 46 - Shri Rama drives on the forest', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 47 - Those who have followed Prince Rama find themselves alone, Shri Rama is the same figure as Rama, revered and deeply missed by the citizens of Ayodhya. His departure has left the people overwhelmed with grief and unable to find him. The citizens lament his absence, calling him full-chested and large-eyed. They are filled with sorrow and question the purpose of their devotion now that he has left them.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 47 - Those who have followed Prince Rama find themselves alone', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 49 - The chariot crosses the boundary of Koshala, Shri Rama is a prince who remembers his father\u2019s command and journeys through the night, crossing the southern boundary of Koshala. He is described as erudite, compassionate, and self-controlled, and he engages in conversation with Sumantra about his future return from the forest. He is also referred to as the Prince of Light.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 49 - The chariot crosses the boundary of Koshala', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 50 - Guha, the chief of ferrymen, Shri Rama is the mighty warrior and protector of dharma, who leaves Ayodhya after fulfilling his father\u2019s command. He is described as a righteous and ascetic figure, living on fruit and roots, and is welcomed by Guha, the chief of ferrymen, with great reverence.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 50 - Guha, the chief of ferrymen', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 51 - The night is spent on the bank of the sacred river, Shri Rama is the chief heir of Dasaratha, a virtuous and beloved prince. He is described as sleeping on a bed of straw in the forest, and his absence is feared to cause the king's death. He is considered the Upholder of Truth and the rightful heir to the throne.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 51 - The night is spent on the bank of the sacred river', 'According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 52 - Sumantra is ordered to return, Shri Rama is the virtuous prince who is ordered to enter the forest with Sita and Lakshmana. He is addressed by Sumantra and Guha, and he instructs them on their respective duties. He is described as a lion among men, a devoted son, and a ruler who obeys his father\u2019s commands. He crosses the Ganga with Sita and Lakshmana and begins his life in the forest as an ascetic.': 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 52 - Sumantra is ordered to return', \"According to Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 53 - Rama, Sita and Lakshmana enter upon exile, Shri Rama is the Delighter of the World, who enters exile with Sita and Lakshmana. He performs evening devotion and addresses Lakshmana about their situation in the forest. He reflects on his mother's grief and the injustice of his exile, while upholding righteousness. He is destined to follow his path despite personal sorrow.\": 'Ithihasa->Ramayana->Ayodhya Kanda->Chapter 53 - Rama, Sita and Lakshmana enter upon exile'}"}]}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 6.33, Rama is the hero and husband of Sita, known for his valor, strength, and righteousness. He is described as a knower of sastras, a protector of all, and a tiger among men. Rama has crossed the ocean with his army and is stationed on the seashore near Lanka.", "chunk_ref_id": 45210, "score": 0.29, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->6.33", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}, {"text": "According to Valmiki Ramayana, 5.36, Rama is the noble prince and husband of Sita. He is described as the knower of the self, a righteous and valiant warrior who has given up sovereignty for righteousness. He is deeply devoted to Sita and is expected to arrive with an army to rescue her.", "chunk_ref_id": 43203, "score": 0.28, "reference": "Valmiki Ramayana->5.36", "is_starred": false, "source_title": "Dhara Facts, Srimad Valmiki Ramayana (English Translation with Commentaries), V.V. Subba Rao & Prof. P. Geervani (Eds.), IIT Kanpur Ramayana Project, Rashtriya Sanskrita Vidyapeetham, Tirupati, 2000", "source_url": "https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/", "source_type": "augmentation", "chunk_misc": []}]}
{"type": "kg_retrieval", "item_id": 2, "data": {"query": "who is rama", "answer": "- Rama vows to destroy Bali (He also kills Kabandha and vows to destroy Bali.)\n- Rama kills Kabandha (He also kills Kabandha and vows to destroy Bali.)\n- Rama defeats Marica (Rama is the central hero who defeats numerous rakshasas, including Shurpanakha, Khara, Dushana, Trishira, and Marica.)\n- Rama defeats Trishira (Rama is the central hero who defeats numerous rakshasas, including Shurpanakha, Khara, Dushana, Trishira, and Marica.)\n- Rama defeats Dushana (Rama is the central hero who defeats numerous rakshasas, including Shurpanakha, Khara, Dushana, Trishira, and Marica.)\n- Rama defeats Khara (Rama is the central hero who defeats numerous rakshasas, including Shurpanakha, Khara, Dushana, Trishira, and Marica.)\n- Rama defeats Shurpanakha (Rama is the central hero who defeats numerous rakshasas, including Shurpanakha, Khara, Dushana, Trishira, and Marica.)\n- Rama returns to Return to Capital (He remains steadfast in obedience to his father's commands and later returns to reclaim his kingdom.)\n- Rama reclaims Throne\n- Rama exiled from Exile (Rama, the son of King Dasaratha and the rightful heir, goes into exile after being forced by his mother Kaikeyi.)\n- Ravana defeats Rama\n- Kishkindhya travels to Rama (where Rama and Sugriva traveled together)\n- Sugriva tests Rama (He shared details of his suffering under Bali and tested Rama's power before fully trusting him.)\n- Sugriva allies with Rama (Sugriva is the monkey king who formed a bond of friendship with Rama after hearing Rama's tale.)\n- Shabari worships Rama (Rama visited her and was duly worshipped by her.)\n- Kabandha killed by Rama (Kabandha is a male asura with a terrifying appearance whom Rama slew after encountering him while searching for Sita.)\n- Trishira killed by Rama (He was among those slain by Rama in battle.)\n- Dushana killed by Rama (He was killed during the confrontation with Rama.)\n- Khara killed by Rama (He participated in the battle where he was ultimately slain by Rama.)\n- Khara battles Rama (He participated in the battle where he was ultimately slain by Rama.)\n\nSources: 48519377-fd49-4128-8937-c7db08316158, d159f6f2-62d7-4c9a-8c31-520588ff8a6c, 1ce1ff42-60d3-4f4b-b238-9d7623f98217, 77ba08af-c183-48b9-81ac-6742e9be1153, f1f30e6e-eafe-48a8-8189-50c5f66d77cb", "strategy_used": "neighborhood", "confidence": 0.7, "graph_results": [{"source": "Rama", "relation": "VOWS_TO_DESTROY", "target": "Bali", "target_id": "ab8fa8ea-1725-4b6f-ad66-66a1b957de2a", "target_labels": ["DEMON"], "evidence": "He also kills Kabandha and vows to destroy Bali.", "chunk_id": "48519377-fd49-4128-8937-c7db08316158"}, {"source": "Rama", "relation": "KILLS", "target": "Kabandha", "target_id": "63be0aaa-2899-4626-8cc7-b8aba1ae2891", "target_labels": ["DEMON"], "evidence": "He also kills Kabandha and vows to destroy Bali.", "chunk_id": "48519377-fd49-4128-8937-c7db08316158"}, {"source": "Rama", "relation": "DEFEATS", "target": "Marica", "target_id": "d7c1a844-716c-4b12-9b3b-badb19d89d3c", "target_labels": ["DEMON"], "evidence": "Rama is the central hero who defeats numerous rakshasas, including Shurpanakha, Khara, Dushana, Trishira, and Marica.", "chunk_id": "48519377-fd49-4128-8937-c7db08316158"}, {"source": "Rama", "relation": "DEFEATS", "target": "Trishira", "target_id": "7ebfb2a9-c17a-4341-9e00-78711595de8f", "target_labels": ["DEMONG"], "evidence": "Rama is the central hero who defeats numerous rakshasas, including Shurpanakha, Khara, Dushana, Trishira, and Marica.", "chunk_id": "48519377-fd49-4128-8937-c7db08316158"}, {"source": "Rama", "relation": "DEFEATS", "target": "Dushana", "target_id": "c5cd9930-fe2f-4372-8534-acb0595d0104", "target_labels": ["DEMONG"], "evidence": "Rama is the central hero who defeats numerous rakshasas, including Shurpanakha, Khara, Dushana, Trishira, and Marica.", "chunk_id": "48519377-fd49-4128-8937-c7db08316158"}, {"source": "Rama", "relation": "DEFEATS"